UPDATE tools SET description = $x$UX Pilot is an AI design tool that generates and edits UI screens, runs UX research and attention prediction, and turns designs into HTML. It works in the browser and inside Figma through its Nodey plugin, using a credit-based freemium model.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'uxpilot' AND lang = 'en';

UPDATE tools SET description_long = $x$UX Pilot is an AI-assisted product design tool aimed at UX/UI designers who want to move from idea to high-fidelity screens quickly. Instead of starting from a blank canvas, you describe what you need and the tool generates full interfaces you can refine.

There is no public model version number. UX Pilot offers multiple generation modes, including a Deep Design mode, that trade higher credit cost for more elaborate output. Through 2026 the product added the Nodey plugin for generating and editing components directly inside Figma, AI UX-research with attention-prediction, and design-to-code that converts an image into HTML. It now runs direct comparison pages against Anthropic's Claude Design and other AI design tools.

Key capabilities include text-to-UI generation, in-Figma editing via Nodey, attention heatmaps and UX-research feedback, and image-to-HTML export for handing work to developers.

Pricing is credit-based. The Free plan is $0 with 45 one-time credits (not a recurring monthly allowance), covering roughly 7 screens, and requires no card. Standard is a paid entry tier with about 420 credits (roughly 70 screens); UX Pilot's own site lists conflicting monthly rates for this tier, so no verified USD price is given here. Pro costs $29/month, or $22/month billed annually ($264/year, saving 25%), with 750 credits, top-up packs, a website cloner, and a 7-day Pro trial, and is marked Popular. Teams costs $39/user/month, or $31/user/month billed annually ($372/user/year, saving 20%), with 900 credits per user drawn from a shared pool. Enterprise is custom, with custom or unlimited credits, SSO via SAML/SCIM, and custom AI model fine-tuning.

The main limitations are the credit economy and inconsistent published pricing: credits do not roll over, the free tier's 45 credits are a one-time grant rather than monthly, the Deep Design mode costs more credits per screen than the standard mode, and the vendor's own site quotes several different monthly figures for the Standard tier, so its true cost should be confirmed before purchase.

It suits designers and small teams who want fast AI-generated screens, Figma users who want generation inside their existing workflow, and anyone who needs quick design-to-HTML output. Teams with steady high-volume needs should model the credit burn, and double-check Standard tier pricing directly with the vendor, before committing.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'uxpilot' AND lang = 'en';

