UPDATE tools SET description = $x$Make is a visual, no-code automation platform for building workflows across 3,000+ apps, with Make AI Agents, a feature currently in beta for building autonomous AI agents inside scenarios.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'make' AND lang = 'en';

UPDATE tools SET description_long = $x$Make (formerly Integromat) is a visual automation platform where you connect apps and services on a drag-and-drop canvas to build workflows, called scenarios, without writing code. Each scenario is a chain of modules that pass data from one app to the next, with branching, filters, iterators and error handling.

Make's billing is credit-based, where the operations a scenario consumes are measured in credits, and an AI or code step can cost more than a single credit. The platform also offers Make AI Agents, currently labeled beta on Make's own pricing page, which let you build autonomous agents directly inside scenarios, backed by an AI Agents Library.

On the capability side, Make advertises 3,000+ app integrations, real-time and scheduled triggers, webhooks, HTTP/API modules, data stores, and reusable custom variables. AI Agents can run on Make's own built-in AI provider on any plan, or you can connect your own LLM key instead.

Pricing is tiered. Free is $0/mo with 1,000 credits, a cap of 2 active scenarios and a 15-minute minimum scheduling interval. Core is $9/month billed monthly, dropping to about $7.65/month if billed annually, with 10,000 credits, unlimited scenarios, a 1-minute interval and Make API access. Pro is $16/month billed monthly, about $13.60/month annually, adding priority execution, custom variables and full-text execution log search. Teams is $29/month billed monthly, about $24.65/month annually, adding team roles and shared scenario templates. Enterprise is custom-priced. Once you exceed plan limits, extra credits are sold in fixed bundles of 1,000 or 10,000 at a price tied to your subscription.

The main limitations are practical. Credit-based pricing makes AI-heavy scenarios harder to forecast, the free tier's 2-scenario cap and 15-minute interval are tight for real work, the lower per-month prices require committing to annual billing, and the visual model still has a learning curve once scenarios grow complex with nested routers and error handlers.

Make suits people who want a powerful visual automation tool without code, and who now want to layer AI agents into those automations rather than glue together separate services.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'make' AND lang = 'en';

